WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:13.910
JULIA SILGE: OK, let's get
started with this model

00:00:13.910 --> 00:00:16.290
monitoring example.

00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:21.360
So I'm going to open up a new R
Markdown file from a template.

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:23.210
So I've got all these
different templates

00:00:23.210 --> 00:00:26.610
from the packages I
have installed here.

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:29.750
And I am going to
use a Flex Dashboard.

00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:32.420
I really like the
Flex Dashboard package

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:34.160
for a lot of
different situations,

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:40.910
like making really pretty
shiny apps without much effort.

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:44.450
Two, dashboards I don't
want to use Shiny with,

00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:47.725
but I want like a dashboard
kind of situation.

00:00:47.725 --> 00:00:49.350
So that's what we're
going to use here.

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:50.525
So I am going to--

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:55.690
let me save it.

00:00:55.690 --> 00:00:59.740
I'm going to call it
this monitor.RMD here.

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:01.030
And then the title--

00:01:01.030 --> 00:01:03.220
so I'm going to--
this is going to be

00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:09.650
a dashboard for monitoring
a model for traffic crashes.

00:01:09.650 --> 00:01:11.590
So the data here, the
data in the model,

00:01:11.590 --> 00:01:17.020
predict the probability that
a crash involves an injury.

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:19.570
It's crashes that are
in the city of Chicago

00:01:19.570 --> 00:01:23.300
and the data comes from
the city of Chicago

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:26.590
and we have all this information
about what the crashes are like

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:30.530
and then we predict whether
the crash involved an injury.

00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:34.040
So Flex Dashboard, we
have all these different--

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:36.760
it is a very flexible tool.

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:39.250
You can do columns or rows.

00:01:39.250 --> 00:01:41.110
I am going to
actually change this

00:01:41.110 --> 00:01:46.730
out and make this the
storyboard mode here,

00:01:46.730 --> 00:01:48.560
which is pretty nice.

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:50.680
And then it turns
out that almost all

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:52.540
of the logic for
the model monitoring

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:54.550
is going to go here
in the setup chunk.

00:01:54.550 --> 00:01:57.190
So we're going to have a really
big setup chunk with lots

00:01:57.190 --> 00:01:59.890
of code, and then
actually less code down

00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:01.850
to create the dashboard itself.

00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:04.300
And when it's time
to monitor something,

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:07.420
I think it's nice to kind of
make clear in your head what

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:09.380
are the pieces that you need.

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:12.400
And so one piece that
you need is your model.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:15.760
So your model is
deployed somewhere.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:18.970
In this case, it's a model
that I already trained.

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:22.000
I have another video that
shows how I trained it.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:26.890
It's deployed using a plumber
API on RStudio Connect.

00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:29.140
But really, that model
could be anywhere.

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:32.030
You just need to be able to get
to it from R, which, of course,

00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:34.870
we have tons of options
for being able to do that.

00:02:34.870 --> 00:02:37.820
And then you need to
get your new data.

00:02:37.820 --> 00:02:40.270
So the new data that's
coming in that you

00:02:40.270 --> 00:02:45.080
want to be able to see how is my
model doing with this new data.

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:48.070
And then your metrics,
you know, the metrics

00:02:48.070 --> 00:02:50.470
that you had back
at the testing time

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:57.280
and then the metrics that we
would measure over time as we

00:02:57.280 --> 00:02:59.990
continue to move forward.

00:02:59.990 --> 00:03:03.550
So these are the pieces
that we need to get going.

00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:06.160
Let us talk about how
to get these things.

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:09.520
So to get the data,
often the easiest thing

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:12.205
is just to go back to when
you trained the model.

00:03:16.370 --> 00:03:20.410
This is the GitHub repo where
I have all the code I used

00:03:20.410 --> 00:03:22.760
to train the model originally.

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:26.110
And so let's just go to the
beginning of that whole script

00:03:26.110 --> 00:03:30.280
and get the code that I
used to train the model.

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:33.860
But instead of getting
two years of data,

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:35.950
let's just get
two weeks of data.

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:40.450
What will happen is as I run
this dashboard week after week

00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:44.650
after week, it will get
newer and newer crashes

00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:45.672
as time passes.

00:03:45.672 --> 00:03:47.380
So let's run this and
make sure it works.

00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:50.890
So what this is doing is
going to the city of Chicago,

00:03:50.890 --> 00:03:54.460
getting the new
crash data, and we

00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:57.850
have the information about
the weather condition,

00:03:57.850 --> 00:04:00.430
the trafficway type
and whatnot, and then

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:03.380
whether there was
an injury or not.

00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:05.980
So that's one piece,
we need the new data.

00:04:05.980 --> 00:04:08.440
The next piece we need
is we need the model.

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:13.240
So to handle the model, we're
going to use the httr package,

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:17.380
we're going to use jsonlite.

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:21.940
The model, in this case,
is deployed as a plumber

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:24.850
API on RStudio Connect.

00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:26.740
It doesn't have to
be this way, right?

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:29.080
Like, wherever your
model is published,

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:31.090
you can go and
get it from there.

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:35.650
But this is how I'm going to
do it here to demonstrate.

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:39.400
So this model has
a predict endpoint.

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:42.760
It's got a metrics endpoint
that are the testing

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:45.590
metrics that we expect to see.

00:04:45.590 --> 00:04:48.230
So I'm going to
copy this URL here.

00:04:48.230 --> 00:04:50.140
And then what I'm
going to do is I'm

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:58.120
going to set up a post call, and
I am going to send as the body,

00:04:58.120 --> 00:04:59.800
I'm going to-- it's this crash.

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:02.170
This crash data is
what I'm going to send.

00:05:02.170 --> 00:05:06.200
But I need to make it to json--

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:10.780
oh, you know what, I don't
think I've loaded these yet.

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:11.320
There we go.

00:05:11.320 --> 00:05:13.330
OK, so to JSON.

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:14.380
And I'm going to do--

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:16.420
I'm going to send
the crash, I'm not

00:05:16.420 --> 00:05:19.010
going to send the
injuries, though.

00:05:19.010 --> 00:05:22.010
And then-- so that would
give me the result.

00:05:22.010 --> 00:05:31.970
And then I want
the text as UTF-8.

00:05:31.970 --> 00:05:34.650
And that will be
JSON at that point.

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:37.370
So I'm going to do fromJSON.

00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:42.690
So these are
predictions like so.

00:05:45.310 --> 00:05:45.920
Like that.

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:47.420
So let's run this.

00:05:47.420 --> 00:05:49.770
So these now are
our predictions.

00:05:49.770 --> 00:05:53.960
So it's sending for all of
those new crashes that I have,

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:56.590
that I want to use for
monitoring, it's sending them

00:05:56.590 --> 00:05:59.680
to the model, getting
predictions for them,

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:04.910
and then handling all of the
output here and getting back.

00:06:04.910 --> 00:06:07.630
So what we have
at the end there--

00:06:07.630 --> 00:06:09.640
let me do it like this--

00:06:09.640 --> 00:06:12.170
is a vector.

00:06:12.170 --> 00:06:12.770
Like this.

00:06:12.770 --> 00:06:13.640
So it's a vector.

00:06:13.640 --> 00:06:17.840
And these are probabilities
of having injuries.

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:21.620
We have our new crash
data, we have our model,

00:06:21.620 --> 00:06:25.550
and then let's put it
together and find our metrics.

00:06:25.550 --> 00:06:31.310
So we take that crash
data and let's find on--

00:06:31.310 --> 00:06:32.840
let's call it pred_insjuries.

00:06:36.530 --> 00:06:38.940
So we just bind
that on together.

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:42.740
So now we have a column of
predicted probabilities.

00:06:42.740 --> 00:06:50.000
Let's make a-- let's make
a column of hard class--

00:06:54.090 --> 00:06:56.650
hard class predictions.

00:06:56.650 --> 00:07:05.970
So if this thing that I just
made is greater than 0.5,

00:07:05.970 --> 00:07:08.730
let's call it injuries.

00:07:08.730 --> 00:07:11.190
And if it's not,
let's call it none.

00:07:11.190 --> 00:07:13.680
So this now matches these.

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:17.100
So I'm making a hard
class prediction.

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:21.420
I think I need to say if
anything is a character,

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:24.310
I need to change it to a factor.

00:07:24.310 --> 00:07:24.810
Right.

00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:25.590
There we go.

00:07:25.590 --> 00:07:26.310
OK.

00:07:26.310 --> 00:07:30.450
So because the function
that I'm about to use

00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:32.730
needs factors and
those functions

00:07:32.730 --> 00:07:35.680
are from the Yardstick package.

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:39.870
So the yardstick package
is from tidymodels.

00:07:39.870 --> 00:07:44.490
And you can use it
with models that

00:07:44.490 --> 00:07:45.900
have been trained
with tidymodels

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:48.900
or you can use it with
any other kinds of models

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:50.910
and get out these nice results.

00:07:50.910 --> 00:07:54.630
Actually, before I started
working for RStudio

00:07:54.630 --> 00:07:56.970
or using tidymodels
at all, I used--

00:07:56.970 --> 00:08:00.150
Yardstick was actually the first
part of this whole ecosystem

00:08:00.150 --> 00:08:02.820
that I used at all
because it is so nice.

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:05.640
And so we can do metrics.

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:11.790
And the way we do it is we say,
the true value is in injuries.

00:08:11.790 --> 00:08:16.530
And then we can say
predicted and pred_injuries,

00:08:16.530 --> 00:08:18.150
so that it's the
hard class and then

00:08:18.150 --> 00:08:20.100
the class probabilities
like this,

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:23.850
and it gives us accuracy, ROC.

00:08:23.850 --> 00:08:26.040
And you look at this,
you're like, is this bad?

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:29.490
Is this worse than what
we expect based on how

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:31.320
the things did at testing?

00:08:31.320 --> 00:08:32.610
This is what we want to know.

00:08:32.610 --> 00:08:35.669
We probably want to
do a little bit more

00:08:35.669 --> 00:08:37.919
because this is for
that whole two weeks.

00:08:37.919 --> 00:08:41.250
And we probably want to add
a little bit more in here

00:08:41.250 --> 00:08:44.430
and say, mutate--

00:08:44.430 --> 00:08:46.860
so we've got to--
what's it called?

00:08:46.860 --> 00:08:48.570
crash_date.

00:08:48.570 --> 00:08:49.680
crash_date.

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:52.350
So we want to do a little
bit of some rounding here.

00:08:52.350 --> 00:08:54.720
Actually, let's do floor_date.

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:59.370
Say, as.date, crash_date.

00:08:59.370 --> 00:09:01.710
And then we're going to
say unit equals week.

00:09:01.710 --> 00:09:04.050
So now we're
rounding by the week

00:09:04.050 --> 00:09:06.060
that we have and
then we're just going

00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:08.730
to group by that crash-date.

00:09:08.730 --> 00:09:10.480
Like this.

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:12.150
So now we have it
for these-- we've

00:09:12.150 --> 00:09:15.460
got three beginnings of
weeks in this data set.

00:09:15.460 --> 00:09:18.490
So we have these
different values here.

00:09:18.490 --> 00:09:26.890
So these are the metrics_by_week
like this that we have.

00:09:26.890 --> 00:09:30.540
And if we are starting
out our model metrics,

00:09:30.540 --> 00:09:34.860
let's say, we just trained our
model and we are starting out,

00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:37.290
we would probably take this.

00:09:37.290 --> 00:09:42.030
Let's say, we train our
model on March 20th.

00:09:42.030 --> 00:09:44.670
We would start this and
it would be time for us

00:09:44.670 --> 00:09:46.230
to start keeping track of this.

00:09:46.230 --> 00:09:50.250
And I think the a great way to
do this is the pins package.

00:09:53.470 --> 00:09:55.150
I'm using the
development version

00:09:55.150 --> 00:09:57.800
of pins as of this recording.

00:09:57.800 --> 00:09:59.380
It will go to CRAN soon.

00:09:59.380 --> 00:10:04.353
But if you see me use some
of the functions and things,

00:10:04.353 --> 00:10:05.770
they might look a
little different

00:10:05.770 --> 00:10:08.740
than what's on CRAN right
now, but those functions

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:09.910
will go there soon.

00:10:09.910 --> 00:10:13.330
And so what we want
to do is I actually

00:10:13.330 --> 00:10:18.457
have some stored metrics
in a pin right now.

00:10:18.457 --> 00:10:20.290
And so I'm going to
show you how to get them

00:10:20.290 --> 00:10:21.887
and then how to rewrite them.

00:10:21.887 --> 00:10:23.470
But if you were
starting from scratch,

00:10:23.470 --> 00:10:26.290
you would just start by
writing without reading first.

00:10:26.290 --> 00:10:28.210
The way that this
works is, let's say,

00:10:28.210 --> 00:10:31.900
I have to register a board,
somewhere to store things.

00:10:31.900 --> 00:10:35.290
So in pins, you can
have a board on azure,

00:10:35.290 --> 00:10:36.940
you can have a board on--

00:10:36.940 --> 00:10:40.300
gosh, there's so many--
there's on Google Cloud

00:10:40.300 --> 00:10:44.860
Storage, on GitHub, on Kaggle.

00:10:44.860 --> 00:10:47.680
There's all these different kind
of boards that you can have.

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:51.100
I'm going to show how to use
a board on RStudio Connect.

00:10:51.100 --> 00:11:01.240
So my server is the same thing
as where my model is deployed.

00:11:01.240 --> 00:11:03.010
Here.

00:11:03.010 --> 00:11:05.560
And then I need my key.

00:11:05.560 --> 00:11:06.340
Sys.

00:11:06.340 --> 00:11:15.510
And it is stored in
my .renviron file.

00:11:15.510 --> 00:11:18.465
So I'm getting it out
of there with this here.

00:11:18.465 --> 00:11:21.850
It's a pretty nice
place to store things.

00:11:21.850 --> 00:11:25.440
So I think if I type out
board here, it'll be like,

00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:27.570
it's a pinboard on
our RStudio Connect

00:11:27.570 --> 00:11:29.850
and here are all the things
that are stored there.

00:11:29.850 --> 00:11:32.670
Like, for my co-workers,
RStudio, all these things.

00:11:32.670 --> 00:11:36.790
And I have something
stored there too.

00:11:36.790 --> 00:11:42.640
If I do pin_read, it's called--

00:11:42.640 --> 00:11:43.870
can I spell my own name?

00:11:43.870 --> 00:11:46.150
I don't know.
julia.silge and it's

00:11:46.150 --> 00:11:47.905
called traffic-crash-metrics.

00:11:50.810 --> 00:11:54.190
And if I read that, I can say--
let's call it old_metrics,

00:11:54.190 --> 00:11:56.160
like this.

00:11:56.160 --> 00:12:00.570
So I'm reading the
old metrics here.

00:12:00.570 --> 00:12:04.100
So I've actually been monitoring
this model for a while now.

00:12:04.100 --> 00:12:06.494
But so I've been like,
monitoring, monitoring,

00:12:06.494 --> 00:12:07.610
monitoring.

00:12:07.610 --> 00:12:10.790
And so now what
I can do is I can

00:12:10.790 --> 00:12:15.800
combine the old metrics
with the new metrics

00:12:15.800 --> 00:12:17.390
and then write it back.

00:12:17.390 --> 00:12:19.280
So I think the way to do this--

00:12:19.280 --> 00:12:20.870
old_metrics.

00:12:20.870 --> 00:12:29.490
So I'm going to do like a filter
that this crash_date isn't it.

00:12:33.570 --> 00:12:34.770
Like that.

00:12:34.770 --> 00:12:36.960
And then just bind
them together.

00:12:36.960 --> 00:12:39.480
So this is kind of
a nice way to do it

00:12:39.480 --> 00:12:44.910
so that you don't end up
overwriting things and all

00:12:44.910 --> 00:12:46.890
that kind of thing.

00:12:46.890 --> 00:12:49.510
OK, so if I do
this, there we go.

00:12:49.510 --> 00:12:54.210
So I have taken my old metrics,
I have added on anything

00:12:54.210 --> 00:12:55.650
that I have that's new.

00:12:55.650 --> 00:12:56.160
Yeah.

00:12:56.160 --> 00:12:58.950
So I can-- maybe I should
arrange it so it's the same.

00:12:58.950 --> 00:13:02.050
Metric, crash_date, like that.

00:13:02.050 --> 00:13:03.280
There we go.

00:13:03.280 --> 00:13:05.760
And so I can write this back.

00:13:05.760 --> 00:13:08.580
So we have our old
metrics, my new--

00:13:08.580 --> 00:13:11.940
the metrics by week, and
let's call that new_metrics,

00:13:11.940 --> 00:13:12.750
like this.

00:13:12.750 --> 00:13:16.140
And then I can
take my board and I

00:13:16.140 --> 00:13:19.750
can write to it my new metrics.

00:13:19.750 --> 00:13:22.620
So I have to say where
do I want to write it to.

00:13:22.620 --> 00:13:30.610
So I want to write it to
that same pin, like that.

00:13:30.610 --> 00:13:32.200
And so it's writing back up.

00:13:32.200 --> 00:13:37.500
So what a pin gets you is
versioned ways to write things

00:13:37.500 --> 00:13:42.870
back and forth, which I think
is really super duper nice

00:13:42.870 --> 00:13:44.710
because you can always go back.

00:13:44.710 --> 00:13:48.090
You can also pin inversion
models themselves.

00:13:48.090 --> 00:13:51.060
It's really useful
for model monitoring.

00:13:51.060 --> 00:13:54.730
OK, so, look at that
chunk, it's enormous.

00:13:54.730 --> 00:13:57.240
So much is going on in it.

00:13:57.240 --> 00:14:01.150
But this is like, we have our
new data, we have our model,

00:14:01.150 --> 00:14:02.250
we have our metrics.

00:14:02.250 --> 00:14:05.410
And so now, let's just
make a few visualizations.

00:14:05.410 --> 00:14:09.600
So I think for the
storyboard, I'm just

00:14:09.600 --> 00:14:11.700
going to put like different--

00:14:11.700 --> 00:14:12.700
a few things here.

00:14:12.700 --> 00:14:16.410
So let's, for example,
say, model metrics here.

00:14:16.410 --> 00:14:21.850
And then we'll put this,
we'll say, like, new_metrics,

00:14:21.850 --> 00:14:23.660
because that was all of it.

00:14:23.660 --> 00:14:25.500
And let's say, what do we have?

00:14:25.500 --> 00:14:28.610
The new metrics.

00:14:28.610 --> 00:14:30.500
new_metrics.

